    "https://www.governmentjobs.com/careers/loyno/jobs/5523220/tenure-track-assistant-professor-of-computer-science": {
      "title": "Tenure-track Assistant Professor of Computer Science",
      "description": "Tenure-track Assistant Professor of Computer Science Salary Depends on Qualifications Location Main Campus, New Orleans, LA Job Type 9 Month Faculty Job Number 202200722 Division Academic Affairs Department Computer Science Opening Date 10/20/2026 FLSA Exempt Description Benefits Description The Department of Mathematics and Computer Science invites applications for a 9-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Computer Science. This position is approved to support the growth of our academic programs, including majors in Computer Science, Cybersecurity, and Game Programming. Examples of Duties Teaching: Teach 3 courses per semester ranging from introductory to upper-level core undergraduate classes. Research: Maintain an active research program in your area of expertise, with expectations to publish and engage in undergraduate research. Service & Advising: Provide academic advising to students and participate in departmental and institutional service. Typical Qualifications Minimum Ph.D. in Computer Science or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Preferred: Expertise in Artificial Intelligence (e.g., Natural Language Processing or Computer Vision), along with the ability to teach cybersecurity courses. Physical Requirements Ability to perform job duties with or without reasonable accommodations. Application Materials Cover Letter detailing their academic qualifications, professional experience, and interest in the position, with emphasis on their ability to support curriculum development and contribute to faculty research. Teaching Statement describing their teaching philosophy and including a list of courses they are qualified to teach. Research Statement outlining their research interests, goals, and future plans. Curriculum Vitae (CV) highlighting relevant academic, professional, teaching, and research accomplishments. In accordance with the Campus Security Act as amended, Loyola University publishes annually and distributes to members of the academic community, and upon request to prospective (students/employees), an annual security report . That report includes current policies and procedures of the University Police Department for the reporting of campus crimes, notifying the campus community of occurrence of campus crimes, policies regarding access to campus buildings, the enforcement authority of the University Police Officers and their relationship to local police. University policies on the possession, use and sale of alcohol and illegal drugs are outlined. The Campus sexual Assault Program and policy is described. Campus crime statistics as defined under this law for the last three calendar years are included. A copy of this report may be secured from the (Office of Admissions), (Office of Human Resources), or from the University Police Department. Loyola University New Orleans does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of race, color, gender, sexual orientation, age, religion, disability, veteran's status or national origin in its educational programs or activities, including employment and admissions. At the same time, Loyola cherishes its right to seek and retain personnel who will make a positive contribution to its religious character, goals, and mission in order to enhance the Jesuit, Catholic tradition. We offer a complete benefits package to full-time employees including health care, dental, vision, retirement, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, long-term disability, tuition remission, holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of 30 hours per week over the course of a year, receive certain benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page . Employer Loyola University New Orleans Apply"
    },
    "https://www.governmentjobs.com/careers/loyno/jobs/5552083/assistant-professor-music-history-tenure-track": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Music History, Tenure-Track",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Music History, Tenure-Track Salary Depends on Qualifications Location Main Campus, New Orleans, LA Job Type 9 Month Faculty Job Number 202200738 Division Academic Affairs Department Music Study & Performance Opening Date 11/17/2026 FLSA Exempt Description Benefits Description Assistant Professor in Music History, Tenure-Track, 10-month appointment Loyola University New Orleans seeks a committed teacher, scholar, and colleague who displays experience with pedagogical innovation, breadth of knowledge and interest, and dedication to serving a diverse student body, to join the School of Music and Theatre Professions at Loyola University New Orleans. The successful candidate will teach and oversee/advise music history courses for curricula ranging from vocal and instrumental performance, jazz studies, commercial music, and Hip-Hop & R&B, music therapy, and music education, as well as for the Loyola Core and Honors program. The appointment will be made at the rank of Assistant Professor (tenure-track) and will begin in August 2027. Examples of Duties Duties will include: Teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in music history, drawing from a wide range of musics and using appropriate pedagogical strategies. Maintaining a scholarly record suitable for progressing toward promotion and tenure, and participating in the intellectual life of the School, College, and University, as well as the field in general. Working with the Director of the School of Music and Theatre Professions and others to plan and implement course schedules, new courses and curricula, and curricular changes. Participation in graduate comprehensive exams and administering diagnostic tests for incoming graduate students (MM in Performance). Service as an academic advisor as needed. Participation in School, College, and University meetings, committees, and other activities, as well as maintaining working relationships with faculty and staff in the School, College, and University. Other duties as assigned. Typical Qualifications Ph. D. in musicology (broadly conceived). ABD candidates will be considered, with the expectation that all degree requirements will be completed before hire date. Demonstrated experience teaching a wide range of musics for majors and non-majors, preferably as instructor of record. The ideal candidate will also demonstrate experience with a range of pedagogical strategies to create an effective learning environment for a diverse student body. Evidence of potential for scholarly contributions suitable for advancement on the tenure track. Physical Requirements Ability to perform job duties with or without reasonable accommodations. The School Loyola’s School of Music & Theatre Professions is an academic community made up of artists, scholars, practitioners and students who believe in the transformative power of the arts and who are dedicated to the education of the whole person in the Ignatian tradition. The School houses all of our classical / jazz / commercial music performance, music education, music therapy, music industry, and theatre degrees. The University Loyola University New Orleans is a Jesuit Catholic University that educates diverse leaders dedicated to justice, compassion, service, and academic excellence. Loyola University New Orleans is firmly committed to and reaffirms its policy to align its recruitment practices with its mission of social justice and equity . Loyola University New Orleans is located in beautiful uptown New Orleans on Historic St. Charles Avenue across from Audubon Park. Application To apply, qualified and interested applicants should submit: Letter of application Curriculum Vitae Transcripts (official or unofficial) Names and contact information for three references Portfolio which includes: Professional work and/or scholarly endeavors. One example of scholarly work Teaching Statement describing teaching philosophy, inclusive of including a list of courses and topics for which you have taught or are qualified to teach. Research Statement outlining research interests, goals, and future plans. Ideal, but not required for first round materials submissions: Link to a teaching demonstration video (minimum 15-20 minutes, not to exceed 30) Only electronic applications will be accepted (single/combined PDF attachments containing links). Application review will commence January 8, 2027 and will continue until the position is filled. The successful candidate will start on August 1, 2027. In accordance with the Campus Security Act as amended, Loyola University publishes annually and distributes to members of the academic community, and upon request to prospective (students/employees), an annual security report . That report includes current policies and procedures of the University Police Department for the reporting of campus crimes, notifying the campus community of occurrence of campus crimes, policies regarding access to campus buildings, the enforcement authority of the University Police Officers and their relationship to local police. University policies on the possession, use and sale of alcohol and illegal drugs are outlined. The Campus sexual Assault Program and policy is described. Campus crime statistics as defined under this law for the last three calendar years are included. A copy of this report may be secured from the (Office of Admissions), (Office of Human Resources), or from the University Police Department. Loyola University New Orleans does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of race, color, gender, sexual orientation, age, religion, disability, veteran's status or national origin in its educational programs or activities, including employment and admissions. At the same time, Loyola cherishes its right to seek and retain personnel who will make a positive contribution to its religious character, goals, and mission in order to enhance the Jesuit, Catholic tradition. We offer a complete benefits package to full-time employees including health care, dental, vision, retirement, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, long-term disability, tuition remission, holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of 30 hours per week over the course of a year, receive certain benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page . Employer Loyola University New Orleans Apply"
    },
    "https://www.governmentjobs.com/careers/loyno/jobs/5552089/assistant-professor-music-industry-tenure-track": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Music Industry, Tenure Track",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Music Industry, Tenure Track Salary Depends on Qualifications Location Main Campus, New Orleans, LA Job Type 9 Month Faculty Job Number 202200739 Division Academic Affairs Department Music Industry Opening Date 11/17/2026 FLSA Exempt Description Benefits Description Assistant Professor (9 month, Full-time, Tenure Track) – starting August 1, 2027 Loyola’s Music Industry Studies (“MIS”) program is nationally recognized for excellence and innovation. The School of Music and Theatre Professions (“SMTP”) offers a variety of degrees within the MIS program including, Bachelor of Science in Music Business, Hip Hop and R&B, and Live Event and Audio Production (in development), Bachelor of Arts in Music Industry Studies and Popular and Commercial Music, as well as a Bachelor of Music in Music Industry Studies. Since its inception, the MIS program has distinguished itself from peer institution programs by having a fiercely entrepreneurial ethos and values-based approach to education. We must recognize the challenges of our location—existing outside of a traditional music business hub—while focusing on the opportunities that come along with it. Not only does New Orleans hold unmatched importance in the history of American music, it currently boasts a live music and festival calendar that few (if any) cities can match. As well, New Orleans remains a destination location for recording artists, writing camps, conventions, and events, all of which offer our students experiential learning opportunities that are second to none. Examples of Duties The current Assistant Professor position will be filled through a national search. Qualified applicants should be prepared to undertake the following teaching and non-teaching responsibilities: Potential Teaching Responsibilities: Intro to Music Industry Studies Music Industry Marketing History of the Music Industry Music Industry Internship Mindfulness for the Music Industry Additional Expected Responsibilities: Academic advising undergraduate students in MIS program Further developing Loyola’s internship and externship opportunities Assisting in coordination and execution of alumni relationship initiatives Participating in recruitment and retention activities Serving on College or University Committees Attending mandatory faculty meetings and development retreats Other duties as assigned based on areas of need and expertise Typical Qualifications Qualified applicants must demonstrate a broad-based understanding of the music business and an ability to educate others. A relevant Bachelor's degree is required. All education, academic, technical, business, and leadership experience is relevant and should be included in the application submission materials. Candidates without a masters or terminal degree should be able to demonstrate commensurate professional experience. Physical Requirements Ability to perform job duties with or without reasonable accommodations. The School Loyola’s School of Music & Theatre Professions is an academic community made up of artists, scholars, practitioners and students who believe in the transformative power of the arts and who are dedicated to the education of the whole person in the Ignatian tradition. SMTP houses all of our classical / jazz / commercial music performance, music education, music therapy, music industry, and theatre degrees. The University Loyola University New Orleans is a Jesuit Catholic University that educates diverse leaders dedicated to justice, compassion, service, and academic excellence. Loyola University New Orleans is firmly committed to and reaffirms its policy to align its recruitment practices with its mission of social justice and equity. Loyola University New Orleans is located in beautiful uptown New Orleans on Historic St. Charles Avenue across from Audubon Park. Application Application review will commence January 8, 2027 and will continue until the position is filled. Complete applications will include a letter of application, a current CV, teaching philosophy statement, relevant transcripts, contact information of three professional references, and any other materials the applicant believes should be considered by the search committee. Only electronic submissions in PDF format will be accepted. In accordance with the Campus Security Act as amended, Loyola University publishes annually and distributes to members of the academic community, and upon request to prospective (students/employees), an annual security report . That report includes current policies and procedures of the University Police Department for the reporting of campus crimes, notifying the campus community of occurrence of campus crimes, policies regarding access to campus buildings, the enforcement authority of the University Police Officers and their relationship to local police. University policies on the possession, use and sale of alcohol and illegal drugs are outlined. The Campus sexual Assault Program and policy is described. Campus crime statistics as defined under this law for the last three calendar years are included. A copy of this report may be secured from the (Office of Admissions), (Office of Human Resources), or from the University Police Department. Loyola University New Orleans does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of race, color, gender, sexual orientation, age, religion, disability, veteran's status or national origin in its educational programs or activities, including employment and admissions. At the same time, Loyola cherishes its right to seek and retain personnel who will make a positive contribution to its religious character, goals, and mission in order to enhance the Jesuit, Catholic tradition. We offer a complete benefits package to full-time employees including health care, dental, vision, retirement, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, long-term disability, tuition remission, holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of 30 hours per week over the course of a year, receive certain benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page . Employer Loyola University New Orleans Apply"
    },
    "https://www.governmentjobs.com/careers/loyno/jobs/5551609/assistant-professor-criminology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Criminology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Criminology Salary Depends on Qualifications Location Main Campus, New Orleans, LA Job Type 9 Month Faculty Job Number 202200723 Division Academic Affairs Department Criminology & Justice Opening Date 11/16/2026 FLSA Exempt Description Benefits Description The Department of Criminology and Justice at Loyola University New Orleans invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position to begin in August, 2027. Examples of Duties The tenure-track position will teach a 3-3 course load in both undergraduate and master’s degree programs, in-person and online. In addition to teaching, the tenure-track position includes advising students, active scholarship (including regular publishing and seeking external funding), and committee service. Other duties as assigned. Typical Qualifications A Ph.D. in Criminology, Criminal Justice, or closely related discipline from an accredited institution is required by the time of hire. Research specialties are open, but special consideration will be given to candidates with expertise in any of the following areas: criminal justice policy and reform, cybercrime, gangs and criminal networks, and applied research and grant writing. Teaching statistics/research methods and expanding the current areas of expertise in the department are highly desired qualifications. Physical Requirements Ability to perform job duties with or without reasonable accommodations. Application Materials Application materials include a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, teaching philosophy, and a list of three references. Questions about the position can be directed to Dr. Christopher Torres, Assistant Professor and Chair, at cetorres@loyno.edu . All applications must be submitted through the careers website. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Loyola University New Orleans is firmly committed to and reaffirms its policy to align its recruitment practices with its mission of social justice and equity. In accordance with the Campus Security Act as amended, Loyola University publishes annually and distributes to members of the academic community, and upon request to prospective (students/employees), an annual security report . That report includes current policies and procedures of the University Police Department for the reporting of campus crimes, notifying the campus community of occurrence of campus crimes, policies regarding access to campus buildings, the enforcement authority of the University Police Officers and their relationship to local police. University policies on the possession, use and sale of alcohol and illegal drugs are outlined. The Campus sexual Assault Program and policy is described. Campus crime statistics as defined under this law for the last three calendar years are included. A copy of this report may be secured from the (Office of Admissions), (Office of Human Resources), or from the University Police Department. Loyola University New Orleans does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of race, color, gender, sexual orientation, age, religion, disability, veteran's status or national origin in its educational programs or activities, including employment and admissions. At the same time, Loyola cherishes its right to seek and retain personnel who will make a positive contribution to its religious character, goals, and mission in order to enhance the Jesuit, Catholic tradition. We offer a complete benefits package to full-time employees including health care, dental, vision, retirement, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, long-term disability, tuition remission, holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of 30 hours per week over the course of a year, receive certain benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page . Employer Loyola University New Orleans Apply"
